      "basis": "Prefer James Glasgow-991 as the possible working father of the Easter Newtoun adult John Glasgow-3610 at 40% subjective confidence, through the adult’s possible identity with John Glasgow-1084, the Mid Calder child of 1616. The 1616 index names James as father. The existing 1616-child account already carries the specific later-family attribution to Annah and the proposed 1642 Porteous marriage and 1643 Edinburgh child. The inspected 1649 and 1652 transcripts now place the named father Johne at Easter Newtoune/Easter Newtoun, supporting one adult household. This makes the existing 1616-family comparison a more economical preferred reconstruction than the 1608 route: the latter requires both the 1608 child’s identification with this adult and the identification of his named Mid Calder father with the Quothquan testator John-3296. Neither route is proved. The inherited Annah attribution is not independent corroboration of the new residence reading.",
      "limitations": "The 40% is a combined judgement about James’s fatherhood of the later adult, not the 98% record-defined fatherhood of the 1616 child. The existing 42% childhood-to-adult comparison remains a distinct scoped assessment; these values are not independent measurements and are not mechanically multiplied. Both childhood dates fit an adult father in 1649: about 33 if baptised near birth in 1616, about 41 if in 1608. Neither childhood transcript supplies a residence, occupation or mother. The retained published Edinburgh marriage entry calls the 1642 groom a whipman but supplies no parents or residence to distinguish the candidates. The 1643 child names John and Issobell Porteous; the 1652 Annah transcript names no mother. A household, property, occupation or witness bridge to either childhood is still missing. Another local John, including the retained Linlithgow comparisons, or an unrepresented family remains possible. Robert-4006 remains a father-generation alternative. William-1087 remains a removed unsupported father, not biologically excluded merely by a 1609 baptism. John-1084 and John-3610 may be duplicate accounts, not two proved distinct sons named John; no merge is performed. Mothers remain separately assessed.",
